{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And your fear and your dread, etc.</b> Even though it was already said ״וְיִרְדּוּ בִדְגַת הַיָּם״ etc. (Genesis 1:28), none the less He adds here the fear and the dread, because He permitted killing them and eating from them. And why should the ox go to the slaughter and not resist its owners, were it not that the hand of HaShem did this — for He cast the fear of man upon all living creatures, to whatever he desires he bends them. And since He permitted the killing of living creatures, you might suppose that the killing of man was also permitted; therefore Scripture says ״וְאַךְ אֶת דִּמְכֶם לְנַפְשֹׁתֵיכֶם״ etc. (Genesis 9:5). And the reason He did not permit the eating of meat to man is that any common person is forbidden to eat meat, but Noah, who engaged in Torah, it was permitted to him. And the commentators gave a reason for the matter: because every creature is nourished from what is beneath it. For the inanimate is the lowest of all existent beings; it is nourished from its own substance. And the plant is nourished from the inanimate, for its nourishment is drawn from the earth. And every living creature is nourished from the plant, and man, of the speaking species, is nourished from living creatures. And these matters apply when he engages in Torah and in his perfection, on account of which he is called man (<i>adam</i>); for without this he is likened — נִדְמָה — to the beasts, and why should he eat his peer? And what perfection is added to his peer when he eats it? For every food is transformed into the nature of the one nourished; and man, when he eats of living creatures, perfection is added to the animal so as to enter the rungs of the speaking species. But a man who is likened to the beast — what can he give and what can he add to that which is eaten? And in this manner it is said (Deuteronomy 7:16) ״וְאָכַלְתָּ אֶת כָּל הָעַמִּים״, and it is written (Numbers 14:9) ״כִּי לַחְמֵנוּ הֵם״, for you are called man (<i>adam</i>) and not the nations."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The phrase וְהַמְפָרְשִׁים (\"the commentators\") is generic; no specific names are given by the Kli Yakar in this passage. The word נִדְמָה in the phrase נִמְשַׁל כַּבְּהֵמוֹת נִדְמָה echoes Psalms 49:13; left untranslated as in the source to preserve the scriptural allusion."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:45.223913298Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}